# FINAL MEGA PROMPT v2 — Moonframe Online Audit → TODO.md + Incremental Releases + Local Coding-Agent Execution Prompt

**Target repository:** https://github.com/bjo163/moonframe  
**Role of the current AI:** Online research/audit orchestrator and local-execution-prompt author.  
**Role of the next AI:** Local coding agent with repository checkout, terminal, build/test tools, and browser capability where available.  
**Primary outcome:** A repository-aware, evidence-based, execution-ready prompt for the local coding agent.  
**Important boundary:** The current online AI must not start implementing the UI product. It audits, researches, reconciles the specification where justified, and writes the next prompt. The local AI will perform implementation after receiving that prompt.

---

## 0. EXECUTIVE MISSION

You are the senior technical lead for Moonframe UI. You are responsible for transforming the accumulated design-system research into a safe, properly sequenced implementation plan that a local coding agent can execute without guessing, duplicating work, or rewriting previously agreed contracts.

The user has run ten deep-research prompt tracks, covering:

1. Token Architecture & Theme Engine Bible
2. Component API & Behavior Bible
3. Premium Blocks & Template Product Bible
4. Advanced Data Workspace Bible
5. Auth & Security UI Integration Bible
6. Original Asset & Brand Bible
7. Motion, Interaction & SFX Bible
8. Accessibility & Visual QA Bible
9. Package Architecture & Developer Experience Bible
10. Master Integration Audit & GitHub Issue Bible

These tracks may have produced substantial documentation, contracts, schemas, research logs, delivery reports, and GitHub issues. **Do not assume all ten tracks are complete, mutually consistent, or correctly implemented. Verify every claim against the current repository.**

Your job is not to create an eleventh broad research Bible. Your job is to determine the true current state, close any specification/CI blocker that prevents safe implementation when feasible, research remaining decisions, and then produce a single comprehensive but staged **Local Coding Agent Execution Prompt**.

The local prompt must tell the local AI exactly what to inspect, what to implement first, what it must not touch, how to test, what evidence to record, how to use existing GitHub issues, and where to stop if a real blocker remains.


## 0.1 MANDATORY FINALIZATION — TODO.md, MILESTONES, RELEASES, AND PARALLEL ADOPTION

These requirements are **mandatory additions** to the earlier online-to-local handoff workflow. They override any weaker output wording elsewhere in this prompt.

The online AI must not return only a report and a local-agent prompt. It must create a usable **master `TODO.md`** and a release-aware execution plan from the verified repository state.

### A. Required primary outputs

1. **Repository audit and source-of-truth reconciliation** based on actual current GitHub state.
2. **`TODO.md` at repository root**: a comprehensive but readable list of small, independently verifiable tasks. It must be useful to a human and to another AI agent without requiring the full chat history.
3. **Milestone and incremental release plan**: every phase/milestone must end with a release decision and an adoptable artifact or an explicitly recorded blocker. A release may be documentation/specification-only, internal preview, local package preview, prerelease, release candidate, or stable product release. Never present a docs-only milestone as a published software release.
4. **Dependency and parallelization map**: tasks that can proceed concurrently, tasks that must remain serial, file ownership/conflict warnings, and integration checkpoints.
5. **Local coding-agent execution prompt**: the final deliverable remains a copy-and-paste-ready prompt, written to a Markdown file in the repo when possible and adapted to the live repository.
6. **Adoption plan**: describe how a consumer/demo app can adopt each meaningful software milestone while implementation continues, without consuming unstable internal files or waiting for every skin/feature to be complete.

### B. `TODO.md` is required, not optional

Create or update `TODO.md` in the root of `bjo163/moonframe`. First inspect whether an existing canonical TODO/backlog file already exists. If a repository convention prefers another path, explain it and still make the root `TODO.md` a concise index pointing to the authoritative detailed plan. Do not create another competing backlog.

`TODO.md` must contain:

- current audit date/timezone, exact audited HEAD SHA, branch, and whether the plan matches the current remote HEAD;
- a concise verified status summary: existing, specified-only, implemented-and-tested, blocked, unknown;
- release train overview and milestone table;
- workstreams and dependencies;
- small task IDs (stable, unique and never silently reused);
- task title and concise objective;
- priority P0/P1/P2/P3 and task size target (prefer roughly one focused agent session; if larger, split it);
- dependencies/predecessors and safe parallel group, if any;
- intended files/areas, clearly labeled as expected rather than guaranteed when further inspection is required;
- acceptance criteria, exact validation/test command when the stack is verified (otherwise a validation plan, not an invented command);
- definition of done and evidence required;
- milestone/release target;
- issue status as verified at audit time or an issue link; never fabricate issue numbers;
- explicit status (`planned`, `ready`, `in-progress`, `blocked`, `done`) only when there is evidence, and a note that live GitHub issue status supersedes a stale mirrored status;
- risk, rollback/migration consideration, and artifact to adopt/test at the end of the task or milestone.

Keep `TODO.md` scan-friendly. Put detailed descriptions, research, schema and long acceptance criteria in canonical documents or GitHub issues; link to them from `TODO.md`. Do not copy the entire contents of every Bible into it.

If the repository already uses `docs/backlog/*.md`, treat those as detailed catalogs only if that is what the live audit confirms. Establish a clear ownership rule: `TODO.md` = top-level ordered execution map; existing detailed backlog files = atomic task specs by domain; GitHub issues = live task state once issues exist. If a GitHub issue and `TODO.md` disagree about whether work is open/closed, trust live GitHub state, reconcile the mirror and report the correction.

### C. Every phase/milestone must include a release/adoption gate

For every milestone, specify:

- milestone ID and goal;
- entry criteria and exact prerequisite tasks;
- an independently verifiable **release artifact**;
- validation gates and required evidence;
- version/tag recommendation, if appropriate, with naming policy;
- changelog/release notes and migration notes;
- known limitations and compatibility promise;
- rollback/revert strategy;
- who/what can adopt it (demo consumer, internal Moon app, local package tarball, registry/source install, docs-only reviewer);
- what is deliberately excluded until a later milestone;
- decision: `release`, `prerelease`, `docs-only checkpoint`, or `blocked`; never call a milestone released without evidence.

Do not assume the user wants immediate public npm publication, or that npm package scope/license/domain rights have been approved. The first adoption path may be a local workspace or packed tarball consumed by a sample app. Public release is an owner-approved decision. Use tags/releases only when justified by the current package/distribution ADR.

### D. Release incrementally; make adoption possible before everything is done

Design the plan so a useful slice is delivered at each milestone. It is **not acceptable** to make one giant v1.0 release depend on all 15 skins, all optional modules, all assets, all SFX, and every premium template being finished.

At minimum, evaluate a sequence like this and adjust it to evidence/current ADRs:

- **M0 — Repository/contract trust:** CI and spec validation are reliable; `TODO.md`, source-of-truth map, current issue dependencies and first local prompt are verified. Artifact: auditable specification snapshot, not a software package release.
- **M1 — Package foundation:** minimal package + consumer fixture can install/build/import a placeholder or first safe export. Artifact: local pack/consumer smoke test and development prerelease if the distribution strategy permits.
- **M2 — Token source/generator slice:** small approved fixture values generate deterministic CSS/token types and the consumer fixture can use them. Artifact: generator proof + fixture output; not all production skin tokens yet.
- **M3 — Theme runtime slice:** requested/effective appearance model, persistence/fallback, and one consumer fixture work. Artifact: preview usable internally.
- **M4 — Four-skin proof/Skin Lab:** Classic, Moonpunk, Moonzine and Moonledger × Light/Dark run on the same reference screens. Artifact: internal design-system preview; visual pair status remains unreviewed until evidence is captured/reviewed.
- **M5 — Core component slice:** useful foundational controls work with contract-driven states and tests. Artifact: adoptable local/package prerelease.
- **M6 — Form and overlay slice:** accessible forms, dialog/menu/select/popover/feedback patterns work. Artifact: incremental prerelease and migration notes.
- **M7 — First premium blocks/templates:** login UI (presentation only), onboarding, dashboard, CRUD list/detail/settings and all essential empty/loading/error states. Artifact: example app that can be adopted independently of real auth/domain backend.
- **M8 — Data workspace slice:** Table/DataTable/filters/pagination/selection and a defined optional engine integration. Artifact: data-workspace prerelease or opt-in module; heavy grid features may remain later.
- **M9 — Additional skin rollout:** remaining skins delivered in tested batches, with distinct grammar evidence rather than recolored themes. Artifact: versioned preview packages and updated coverage evidence.
- **M10 — Asset/motion optional packs:** approved original assets and opt-in SFX/motion are shipped only after provenance, licensing, performance and accessibility gates. Artifact: optional assets/effects pack; not forced into core bundle.
- **M11 — Release candidate:** package exports, docs, consumer tests, accessibility/visual QA, migration guide, and limitations reviewed.
- **M12 — Stable release:** publish/tag only when owner approval, package-name/license decisions, versioning and release gates are satisfied.

This is a candidate release train, not a demand to use those exact numbering/version strings. Preserve the principle that **each milestone delivers a verifiable increment** and consumer adoption can start early. If repo evidence calls for another order, explain why and update `TODO.md`, the roadmap, and local prompt consistently.

A phase may end with `blocked` rather than a fake release if a decision/evidence gate cannot be met. Record the reason, blocker owner/decision, recovery task, and whether other independent lanes may continue.

### E. Parallel workstreams: concurrency with explicit boundaries

Identify workstreams that can proceed in parallel only after their interfaces/dependencies are stable. At minimum consider:

- **Lane A — Repo/CI/governance:** validator reliability, issue/backlog reconciliation, docs navigation, release policy.
- **Lane B — Package and consumer fixture:** only after toolchain/support/primitive/distribution choices are evidence-backed enough.
- **Lane C — Token source and validation:** may design/validate fixture contracts while provider evaluation proceeds, but cannot finalize generator outputs before the token-format decision.
- **Lane D — Theme resolver / Skin Lab:** contracts and deterministic fixtures may be planned early, runtime depends on token/source/provider/consumer constraints.
- **Lane E — Component/API proof slice:** public API contract can be refined in parallel, but implementation begins after primitive provider/API decisions are closed.
- **Lane F — Premium blocks and data workspace:** specs/catalog may proceed in parallel; implementation depends on core components and their state/accessibility contracts.
- **Lane G — Assets/motion/SFX:** manifests/briefs/policies can be refined in parallel; production asset/audio work waits for approved skin grammar and explicit phase authorization.
- **Lane H — QA/release infrastructure:** test matrix and release checklist can proceed in parallel; visual baseline pass requires actual deterministic renders.

For each lane document:

- prerequisites and interface contracts;
- files/paths it owns (avoid two agents editing the same file concurrently);
- tasks safe to parallelize;
- tasks that must be serialized;
- merge/integration checkpoint;
- tests required before other lanes consume its output.

Parallelism is not “give 10 agents the same repo and hope.” Avoid conflicting edits to README, master Bible, contract IDs, schemas or validator. Assign file ownership or sequence the integration.

### F. Adoption lane and consumer feedback loop

Define an adoption lane that runs alongside product implementation:

1. Start with a minimal reference consumer app after the foundation can be built safely.
2. Install the package using the intended supported path (workspace, local tarball, or source registry) rather than importing private source paths.
3. Exercise theme selection, one reference page and basic components.
4. At later milestones, add forms/overlays, blocks/templates and data workspace incrementally.
5. Track integration friction: required CSS, Tailwind scanning/config, SSR/hydration behavior, TypeScript exports, peer dependency errors, styles leaking, accessibility regressions and upgrade steps.
6. Feed findings back into developer docs and compatibility tests before expanding public API.

The consumer lane should not need all 15 skins or every template before providing useful feedback. Use a small controlled fixture first, then a realistic adopter app. Avoid inventing real Moonships/Moonradio application versions: use actual consumer evidence if available; otherwise call it a synthetic fixture.

### Product north star

> **One behavioral system. Fifteen genuinely distinct visual grammars. Thirty Light/Dark baseline appearances. A premium-ready component, block, and template system.**

The product is not a simple theme-color switcher. Each of the 15 skins should ultimately alter visual grammar (layout composition, geometry, type hierarchy, surfaces, navigation/data composition, component recipes, media/decorative language, and motion), while preserving component meaning, behavior, keyboard semantics, and accessibility.

### Non-negotiable scope boundary

- The online AI must **not** implement React components, the production theme runtime, UI templates, auth backends, data engines, or production design assets.
- The online AI must not generate production logos, SVG/PNG artwork, icon packs, illustrations, fonts, or SFX.
- The online AI may inspect code/specs, run/read remote validation evidence where supported, do current web research, update or create documentation/contracts only when required to correct a proven conflict, and create the local-execution prompt.
- The local AI will implement in small, dependency-aware slices. The local AI must not try to deliver all 15 skins and every premium feature in one uncontrolled pass.
- Never claim work is complete without evidence from current GitHub state or actual local test output.

